        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          STS-92 marked the 100th mission in the history of the Shuttle Program.  During the STS-92 mission, also called International Space Station (ISS) assembly flight 3A, the Space Shuttle Discovery delivered assembly hardware components to the station.  Discovery docked with the ISS on October 13, 2000 at 12:45 pm CST.  ISS 3A payloads included the Zenith Z1 truss and the third Pressurized Mating Adapter (PMA-3). The orbiter crew included Commander Brian Duffy, Pilot Pamela A. Melroy, and Mission Specialists Leroy Chiao, Michael E. Lopez-Alegria, William S. McArthur, Jr., Peter J. K. (Jeff) Wisoff, and Koichi Wakata.<P>The Z1 truss is an early exterior framework that that contains four large gyroscopic devices, called Control Moment Gyros, which help maneuver the space station into the proper orientation on-orbit, communications systems to support early science capabilities and U.S. television, secondary power distribution, thermal control system hardware, mechanical interfaces, and EVA robotics hardware.  The Z1 fixture also serves as the platform on which the huge U.S. solar arrays are mounted.  <P>PMA-3 will provide a place for an orbiter to dock with the U.S. segment of the ISS.  PMA-3 includes mechanical interfaces, space walk hardware and thermal control equipment, electrical power subsystem and data handling.  PMA-3 will not be used for docking until mission 4A.  The PMA-3 is equipped with a portable foot restraint (PFR) top-mounted worksite interface fixture, which is used to attach PFR workstation stanchions; two flight-releasable grapple fixtures that provide the standard mechanical interface between the shuttle's robotic arm or remote manipulator system and payloads; camera and laser targets, which consist of a camera target on the APAS hatch, a hemispherical laser target on PMA-3, and planar laser reflectors on the side of the APAS; a number of Space Vision System targets; and handholds and handrails that help the EVA crewmembers move about.<P>Astronauts spent a week docked to the space station continuing on-orbit construction and prepared the station for the arrival of the first resident crew.  During the course of four scheduled space walks, two teams of space walkers and an experienced robot arm operator installed the Z1 truss structure on top of the U.S. Unity connecting node and delivered the third Pressurized Mating Adapter to the ISS for future berthing of new station components and to accommodate shuttle dockings.<P>The first EVA was performed on October 15, 2000 lasting 6 hours, 28 minutes.  The objective was to connect electrical umbilicals to provide power to heaters and conduits located on the Z1 truss; relocation and deployment of two communication antenna assemblies; and installation of a toolbox for use during on-orbit communications.  <P>The second EVA was performed on October 16, 2000 and lasted 7 hours, 7 minutes.  Attachment of the PMA-3 to the space station was accomplished, and the Z1 truss was prepared for future installation of the solar arrays that will be delivered aboard STS-97.  <P>The third EVA took place on October 17, 2000 for 6 hours, 48 minutes.  Two DC-to-DC converter units were installed atop the Z1 truss to convert electricity generated by the solar arrays to the proper voltage.<P>The last EVA was performed on October 18, 2000 and lasted 6 hours, 56 minutes.  Objectives for this EVA included testing of the manual berthing mechanism; deployment of a tray that will be used to provide power to the U.S. lab; and removal of a grapple fixture from the Z1 truss.  Two small rescue backpacks that could enable a drifting astronaut to regain the safety of the spacecraft were also tested.<P>On flight day nine, connections for the newly installed Z1 truss external framework were completed, and equipment and supplies were transferred for the first resident crew.  They also completed testing of the four control moment gyros.<P>Detailed Supplementary Objectives (DSOs) were also performed on this mission. A DSO is a NASA-sponsored investigation performed by Space Shuttle crewmembers, who serve as the test subjects. These studies are designed to require minimal crew time, power and stowage. Biomedical DSOs focus on operational concerns, including space motion sickness, cardiovascular deconditioning, muscle loss, changes in coordination and balance strategies, radiation exposure, pharmacokinetics and changes in the body's biochemistry.<P>The Shuttle undocked from the Space Station on October 20, 2000 at 10:08 a.m. CST.  After an additional two days in orbit due to adverse weather conditions, the Shuttle landed at Edwards Air Force Base in California on October 24, 2000.<END>
